# ============================================================================
# InsurRouter / VeloDocs — environment template
# ============================================================================
# Copy to `.env` and fill in real values:   cp .env.example .env
#
# NOTE: nothing in this workspace calls dotenv. A `.env` file is NOT read
# automatically. Load it explicitly with Node's built-in flag:
#     node --env-file=.env ...
# or export the vars in your shell before running a command. See CLAUDE.md
# ("Running locally") for the exact commands.
# ============================================================================


# ─── Required ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Postgres connection string. Read by lib/db/src/index.ts and
# lib/db/drizzle.config.ts; both throw on startup if it is missing.
#
# Use the **session pooler**, not the direct connection. Supabase's direct host
# (db.<ref>.supabase.co) resolves to IPv6 only, so on any network without a
# global IPv6 route both the API server and drizzle-kit hang on connect with no
# error message. The pooler is dual-stack.
#
# Supabase → Project Settings → Database → Connection string → Session pooler
#   postgresql://postgres.<ref>:PASSWORD@aws-<n>-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres
#
# Port 5432 (session mode), not 6543 (transaction mode) — drizzle-kit push
# issues DDL and needs a real session.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres.PROJECTREF:PASSWORD@aws-1-ap-south-1.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres

# The same database as a restricted role, and the api-server refuses to start
# without it.
#
# `postgres` above owns every table and has `bypassrls`, so row-level security
# is inert for it — anything holding that string reads every dealership's
# customers. `ddms_app` owns nothing, bypasses nothing, and has no grants at all
# on `users` or `sessions`, so it cannot read a session token and therefore
# cannot claim to be an owner it is not. The DDMS request path connects as it.
#
# Same host, same database, same port. Change only the user and password. On
# Supabase's pooler the username carries the project ref after a dot, exactly as
# it does above.
#
# Pick any password and put it here first — `pnpm run db:rls` creates the role
# with whatever this says, so this file is the only place it is written down.
DATABASE_URL_APP=postgresql://ddms_app.PROJECTREF:APP_PASSWORD@aws-1-ap-south-1.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres

# Two more restricted roles, for the two jobs that happen with nobody signed in.
#
# `ddms_app` above is scoped by the session on its connection, which means it
# cannot do anything that has no session: working out *who* is signing in, and
# syncing every dealership on a timer. Both used to run as `postgres`, and that
# is the only reason the bypassrls credential was still in the server.
#
#   ddms_login    users, sessions, and the staff master the departure check
#                 joins. Nothing else. It may write exactly one column —
#                 users.last_login_at — so a leak of the credential that can see
#                 password hashes cannot rewrite one or change somebody's role.
#   ddms_worker   the mirror tables and the entity graph, every tenant, because
#                 that is what syncing on a timer is. No users, no sessions, no
#                 applications, nothing a person decided.
#
# Same host, same database. `pnpm run db:rls` creates both with the passwords
# these lines carry, and then proves what each one can and cannot reach.
DATABASE_URL_LOGIN=postgresql://ddms_login.PROJECTREF:LOGIN_PASSWORD@aws-1-ap-south-1.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres
DATABASE_URL_WORKER=postgresql://ddms_worker.PROJECTREF:WORKER_PASSWORD@aws-1-ap-south-1.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres

# ─── Which of the four the API server gets ───────────────────────────────────
# Not this file. `.env` carries DATABASE_URL because drizzle-kit, the seeds and
# db:rls need the table owner — and the API server **refuses to start** with it
# set, because a server holding it is one import away from bypassing every
# policy and the mistake would be a query that silently works.
#
# So the server runs from `.env.api`, which is `.env` without that one line.
# See .env.api.example, and `refuseOwnerCredential` in lib/db/src/scope.ts.


# ─── Per-process: PORT and BASE_PATH ─────────────────────────────────────────
# Every artifact requires PORT, and the web artifacts also require BASE_PATH;
# each throws at startup if its value is missing. The values differ per
# artifact, so they CANNOT all live in one .env — set them inline per command.
#
#   api-server     PORT=8080
#   insur-router   PORT=24791   BASE_PATH=/
#   doc-ingest     PORT=18815   BASE_PATH=/doc-ingest/
#   mockup-sandbox PORT=8081    BASE_PATH=/__mockup
#   rc-capture     PORT=23754   BASE_PATH=/rc-capture/
#
# Port the Vite dev servers proxy "/api" to. Defaults to 8080 if unset.
# API_PORT=8080


# ─── API authentication ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# One shared secret for every /api route except /api/healthz. The api-server
# THROWS AT STARTUP without it — a server that quietly runs unauthenticated is
# the failure this is meant to prevent.
#
# Generate one:
#     node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
#
# The Vite dev servers read this and inject it into their /api proxy, so the key
# stays in Node and never reaches a browser bundle. Send it by hand as either
# `Authorization: Bearer <key>` or `x-api-key: <key>`.
#
# This says "one of our own processes is calling". It does NOT say which
# showroom is calling, so it cannot enforce owner scoping — that is what the
# session cookie and the row policies do, and neither substitutes for the other.
API_SERVICE_KEY=

# Browser origins allowed to call the API, comma-separated. Unset means the
# three local dev servers. Set it explicitly wherever this is deployed: the
# previous `cors()` with no arguments reflected every origin on the internet.
# CORS_ORIGINS=http://localhost:24791,http://localhost:18815,http://localhost:9090


# ─── OCR engines (VeloDocs /doc-ingest) ──────────────────────────────────────
# Engines are tried in the priority order stored in the `ocr_engines` table and
# edited from the VeloDocs "Engines" tab — no restart needed to reorder them.
# Adding or removing a KEY does need a restart, since keys are read from here.
#
# An engine counts as available only when its key is present. With no key set,
# automatic mode returns HTTP 502 listing what it tried; it will NOT silently
# fall back to the stub, because the stub returns fabricated data.
#
# Per-attempt timeout in milliseconds. Default 45000.
# OCR_TIMEOUT_MS=45000
#
# Optional model overrides.
# OPENAI_VISION_MODEL=gpt-4o
# DASHSCOPE_VISION_MODEL=qwen-vl-plus

# ── Engine "gemini" — Google Gemini, native API ──────────────────────────────
# BEST FREE OPTION. The Flash models are free AND multimodal, so image input is
# included rather than an upsell. Key from https://aistudio.google.com/apikey
# (no credit card). Free-tier limits are shown live in your AI Studio dashboard.
# Uses responseMimeType: application/json, so the model is constrained to emit
# valid JSON — the most common parse failure simply cannot happen.
GEMINI_API_KEY=
# Defaults to the moving alias "gemini-flash-latest". Google retires specific
# versions for new accounts WITHOUT removing them from the models list, so a
# pinned version can start 404-ing with "no longer available to new users".
# Only pin one if you need reproducibility.
# GEMINI_VISION_MODEL=gemini-3.5-flash

# ── Engine "openrouter" — aggregator, one key for many models ────────────────
# Key from https://openrouter.ai/keys. Free tier is 50 requests/day and 20/min
# with no payment; a ONE-TIME $10 purchase (never expires) raises the daily cap
# to 1000. Failed attempts still count against the quota.
#
# Free vision-capable models carry a ":free" suffix and the roster rotates —
# check https://openrouter.ai/models with the free + image filters and set the
# model below. If the configured model is retired, OpenRouter returns a clear
# "model not found" and the chain falls through to the next engine.
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
# OPENROUTER_VISION_MODEL=google/gemma-4-31b-it:free
# OPENROUTER_SITE_URL=http://localhost

# ── Engine "qwen-vl" — Alibaba DashScope, model qwen-vl-plus ────────────────
DASHSCOPE_API_KEY=

# The engine dropdown offers "PaddleOCR (requires PADDLEOCR_API_URL)", but that
# branch throws before reading any variable — no code reads PADDLEOCR_API_URL.
# Setting it does nothing until a text-to-MsaFields extractor is written.
# PADDLEOCR_API_URL=

# Engine "gpt-vision" — OpenAI, model gpt-4o.
OPENAI_API_KEY=

# Replit AI-Integrations proxy. If set, these take priority over OPENAI_API_KEY.
# Leave both blank when running outside Replit.
AI_INTEGRATIONS_OPENAI_API_KEY=
AI_INTEGRATIONS_OPENAI_BASE_URL=


# ─── Insurance provider credentials (optional) ───────────────────────────────
# api-executor.ts reads keys by convention: process.env[`${PROVIDER_CODE}_API_KEY`]
# where PROVIDER_CODE is the `code` column of the providers table. Add one line
# per provider you wire up, e.g.:
# HDFC_API_KEY=
# BAJAJ_API_KEY=


# ─── Playwright ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Path to a Chromium binary, used by BOTH browser paths (policy submission in
# browser-executor.ts and portal scraping in ingest.ts).
#
# Leave BLANK off-Replit: Playwright then uses its own downloaded browser, which
# is what you want locally. Install it once with:
#     pnpm --filter @workspace/api-server exec playwright install chromium
#
# On Replit, set this to the Nix Chromium path — that also switches on the
# sandbox/GPU workaround flags that environment needs:
#   /nix/store/qa9cnw4v5xkxyip6mb9kxqfq1z4x2dx1-chromium-138.0.7204.100/bin/chromium
CHROMIUM_EXECUTABLE_PATH=


# ─── RC Capture (Expo mobile app) ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Host the mobile app calls for /api. Injected from REPLIT_DEV_DOMAIN on Replit.
# Its `dev` script is POSIX-only and Replit-specific — see CLAUDE.md.
# EXPO_PUBLIC_DOMAIN=
#
# The device talks to the API directly with no proxy to inject the service key,
# so the key must be bundled. Fine for a dev build on your own phone; NOT fine
# for anything a real user installs — an EXPO_PUBLIC_ value can be read straight
# out of the package. Set it to the same value as API_SERVICE_KEY.
# EXPO_PUBLIC_API_SERVICE_KEY=


# ─── Dealer DMS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The dealership's own system. It is authoritative for every vehicle fact —
# chassis, engine, cc or kW, colour, manufacture month, ex-showroom price — so
# with it connected, OCR narrows to reading the customer's KYC.
#
# For local work, point at the mock in artifacts/dms-mock:
#     $env:DMS_PORT=9090; pnpm --filter @workspace/dms-mock run start
DMS_API_URL=http://localhost:9090
DMS_API_KEY=dev-dms-key

# Per-attempt deadline and total attempts (including the first). A real OEM DMS
# is a shared ERP that returns 503 under load; the client retries 429/502/503/504
# and timeouts, and never retries a 4xx.
# DMS_TIMEOUT_MS=10000
# DMS_MAX_ATTEMPTS=3

# How often the API server re-pulls every active showroom into the mirror.
# Default 900000 (15 minutes); set to 0 to turn the scheduler off. The pull only
# fetches a full record when the list summary shows movement, so a quiet quarter
# hour costs one list call per dealer code.
#
# Without this the mirror is only as fresh as the last person who remembered to
# press Sync — and "nobody has time to check" is the problem being solved.
# DMS_SYNC_INTERVAL_MS=900000
# Delay before the first pass, so a restart does not immediately hit the ERP.
# DMS_SYNC_INITIAL_DELAY_MS=30000

# Mock-only failure injection. Turn these ON while working on the DMS client —
# a client only ever tested against an instant, always-up DMS will fall over the
# first time it meets a real one.
#     $env:DMS_MOCK_LATENCY_MS=1200; $env:DMS_MOCK_FAIL_RATE=0.3


# ─── Logging ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# LOG_LEVEL defaults to "info". NODE_ENV=production disables pretty-printing.
# LOG_LEVEL=debug
# NODE_ENV=development
